des. Because \"Skip nodes already removed\" implies initially none removed, so all nodes are in queue. Then we process. So I'll initialize queue with all nodes (including root? root is not removable, depth 0). Root can be processed, but root is not removable (removable false), so it will be skipped? Actually condition: \"Remove only a removable leaf whose utility is at most the threshold.\" So for root, it's not removable, so it will not be removed. But it will be popped and visited. So root will appear in visit order.\n\nEdge cases: leaf: a node with no children that are currently present (not removed). Children must be checked dynamically because children can be removed. So we need to maintain a set of removed nodes, and for each node, we can compute if it is a leaf by checking if any children (from original structure) are not removed. We need to precompute children mapping. The case payload gives nodes list. We'll build parent->children mapping. Also store node objects keyed by id.\n\nAlgorithm:\n- Parse case: nodes list, utility_threshold.\n- Build dict node_by_id: {id: node_dict}, children: {parent_id: list of child_ids} (sorted maybe). Also root id.\n- Initialize removed = set().\n- Initialize visit_order = [].\n- Initialize heap: push for each node: (-depth, id, node). (depth: node['depth'])\n- While heap:\n  - pop smallest (largest depth, smallest id). Note: python heap returns smallest, so (-depth, id) works: larger depth -> -depth smaller, so popped first. For same depth, smaller id popped first.\n  - node_id = id\n  - visit_order.append(node_id)\n  - if node_id in removed: continue\n  - if not is_leaf(node_id, removed, children): continue? Actually condition requires leaf. If not leaf, skip (do nothing). But spec: \"Remove only a removable leaf\". So if not leaf, we skip (do not remove). The node remains in queue? Actually we popped it, and we are not removing it. But we don't enqueue it again. That's fine. It will not be processed again unless enqueued later via parent addition.\n  - if node is removable (node['removable'] is True) and node['utility'] <= utility_threshold:\n    - remove it: add to removed set.\n    - parent_id = node['parent']\n    - if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n      - push (-node_by_id[parent_id]['depth'], parent_id, node_by_id[parent_id]) onto heap. Note: We should ensure parent is not already removed. Also, we might want to avoid duplicate pushes? But it's okay.\n- After loop, retained = [id for id in node_by_id if id not in removed]\n- Return {\"retained_ids\": retained, \"visit_order\": visit_order} (or maybe other fields? The spec says \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" So I'll output a dict with those keys. Also raise ValueError for infeasible? Not sure what infeasible means. Possibly if there's a cycle or missing parent? We'll assume valid input.\n\nOne nuance: is root considered a leaf? Root has no parent. Root has children. So root is not leaf. So root will never be removed. So root always remains.\n\nAlso, note that after removal, we enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration. That parent might have been already processed earlier. So it gets another chance. That's correct.\n\nImplementation details: use heapq. For tie-breaking, depth descending, then id ascending. We push (-depth, id, node). The heap will pop smallest (-depth, id). That gives largest depth first; for equal depth, smallest id first.\n\nNow we need to output valid JSON with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a string. Escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. Write the solve function.\n\nLet me write the code.\n\nPotential pitfalls: The case payload may have \"_case_nonce\" but we ignore. Also need to handle nodes with parent null for root.\n\nOutput must be exactly one JSON object. So after constructing the source string, we must put it as value for key \"implementation\". Ensure no trailing whitespace.\n\nI'll produce the source code:\n\n```python\nimport heapq\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    # Build maps\n    node_map = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}\n    children = {node['id']: [] for node in nodes}\n    for node in nodes:\n        parent = node['parent']\n        if parent is not None:\n            children[parent].append(node['id'])\n    # Initialize heap\n    heap = []\n    for node in nodes:\n        heapq.heappush(heap, (-node['depth'], node['id'], node))\n    removed = set()\n    visit_order = []\n    while heap:\n        neg_depth, nid, node = heapq.heappop(heap)\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        if nid in removed:\n            continue\n        # Check if leaf: no children that are not removed\n        is_leaf = True\n        for child_id in children.get(nid, []):\n            if child_id not in removed:\n                is_leaf = False\n                break\n        if not is_leaf:\n            continue\n        # Check removable and utility\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold:\n            removed.add(nid)\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id not in removed:\n                parent_node = node_map[parent_id]\n                heapq.heappush(heap, (-parent_node['depth'], parent_id, parent_node))\n    retained = [node['id'] for node in nodes if node['id'] not in removed]\n    return {'retained_ids': retained, 'visit_order': visit_order}\n```\n\nNeed to import heapq inside function? We can import at top of module, but inside the function is fine. But in a restricted environment, maybe no imports? The standard library is allowed. Use import.\n\nI think that's correct.\n\nNow produce JSON string with escaped strings.\n\nWrite: \"{\\\"implementation\\\": \\\"...\\\"}\". We'll need to escape backslashes, newlines, quotes.\n\nLet me construct the string carefully. I'll write the code as a single line with \\n for newlines. But easier: I'll write a Python script to generate the JSON. But I must output directly. I'll manually escape.\n\nCode as above. Let me write it as a raw string then escape. Use triple quotes? But JSON string must be on one line. I'll write in the response.\n\nBetter: I'll use a heredoc-like approach but in JSON. I'll produce the final answer.\n\nNote: The problem statement says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields.\" So the output of solve should be a dict with keys \"retained_ids\" and \"visit_order\". That's fine.\n\nAlso raise ValueError for infeasible. Not needed for given cases.\n\nNow produce final JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1817,"completion_tokens":3253,"total_tokens":5070,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1792},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":2891},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1792,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":25},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}